  "text": "Registered residential sales reached 13,074 units valued at AED 37,743,569,161 in the last four weeks, marking a 12.6 percent increase in volume and a 9.9 percent rise in value compared to the previous period. Mortgage registrations totaled 5,489, recorded separately from sales transactions. The median residential price per square foot climbed to AED 1,734, up 2.7 percent from the prior four-week median of AED 1,688. Off-plan transactions accounted for 56.8 percent of total sales, slightly below the 57.2 percent share seen in the preceding period.\n\nRental activity remained robust with 107,552 Ejari contracts registered over the last four weeks. Renewals represented 51.9 percent of these contracts, while the median annual residential rent stood at AED 68,000. In the secondary market, Dubai South led with 2,708 sales over twelve weeks at a median of AED 1,641 per square foot, showing a 4.1 percent decline. Jumeirah Village Circle followed with 2,526 sales at AED 1,434 per square foot, remaining stable with a 0.2 percent change. Business Bay recorded 1,538 sales at AED 2,146 per square foot, down 1.1 percent.\n\nPrice momentum varied significantly across communities. Al Yufrah 1 saw the strongest heating with a 29.6 percent increase in median price per square foot over twelve weeks, based on 331 benchmark sales. Dubai Industrial City rose 10.4 percent, while Dubai Production City gained 7.2 percent. Conversely, Saih Shuaib 1 cooled by 29.6 percent, and Sama Al Jadaf fell 14.1 percent. Al Hebiah First declined 12.8 percent, and Palm Jumeirah dropped 10.1 percent. Dubai Creek Harbour showed a 5.7 percent increase, supported by 657 benchmark sales.\n\nInvestors should note that International City Phase 1 offers the highest estimated gross yield at 8.6 percent for flats, with a median rent of AED 38,000. Zaabeel First follows at 7.5 percent, and Town Square at 6.9 percent. The anomaly monitor flagged 936 residential sales in the last 60 days priced more than 35 percent away from their comparable cell median. These figures are derived from registered DLD data and Ejari contracts, using medians and benchmark filters that exclude bulk deals, partial shares, and statistical outliers.",
